{
  "beruf": "gastronomie",
  "title": "Hospitality: bars, restaurants and kitchens",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "spanish-for-work run, 2026-11-07: written by hand for Spain, not yet checked by a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon or in another deck are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "La hostelería employs many newcomers in Spain, from the kitchen to the terraza. This deck has the kitchen, the tasks, orders and service, food hygiene, and what you say to guests and to your team. With customers Spanish bars mostly use tú or a friendly usted; older guests usually get usted.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in a bar, restaurant or kitchen in Spain? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Camarero/a",
    "Ayudante de cocina",
    "Friegaplatos",
    "Cocinero/a",
    "Repartidor/a de comida"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "A Spanish kitchen speaks in short shouts. Learn them first: «¡Marchando!», «¡Oído!», «¡Cuidado, que quema!»",
    "With guests, say «¿Qué le pongo?», «¿Qué os pongo?» or «¿Ya saben qué van a tomar?». You don't need long polite sentences: a warm tone does the work.",
    "If a guest asks about allergens and you are not sure, never guess: «Un momento, lo pregunto en cocina.» Restaurants have to be able to give this information.",
    "Food-handler training (manipulador de alimentos) is often asked for. Ask your employer what they need from you. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
